          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.326,
            0.085,
            0.673,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.388,
            0.683,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": "Cover the square on the hypotenuse using your pieces."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.312,
            0.416,
            0.671,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.722,
            0.846,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": "This shows that if a right triangle has shorter sides of length a and b, and hypotenuse of length c, then the areas of the two smaller squares, $a ^ { 2 }$ and $b ^ { \\bar { 2 } } ,$ , add up to the area of the larger square, $c ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.444,
            0.781,
            0.553,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 } = c ^ { 2 } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.806,
            0.845,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "This is the famous and fundamental theorem of Baudhāyana on right-angled triangles:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.861,
            0.828,
            0.919
          ],
          "content": "**Baudhāyana’s Theorem on Right-angled triangles:** If a right-angled triangle has sidelengths a, b, and c, where c is the length of the hypotenuse, then $a ^ { 2 } \\stackrel { \\smile } { + } b ^ { 2 } = c ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.093,
            0.923,
            0.129,
            0.942
          ],
          "content": "46"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
